sentences.t:
Plogo a mio [Çid] e a todos sos vasallos;
“Aun si Dios quisiere e el Padre que está en alto,
amos los mios yernos buenos serán en ca[n]po.”

sentences.l:
It-pleased my [Çid] and all his vassals;
“Yet if God shall-wish and the Father who is on high,
both the my sons-in-law good will-be in fi[e]ld.”

sentences.t:
—“Yo vos digo, Çid, por toda caridad,
que oy los ifantes a mí por amo non abrán;
cúrielos qui quier, ca dellos poco m' incal.

sentences.l:
—“I to-you say, Çid, by all charity,
that today the infantes me for tutor not will-have;
let-guard-them who will, for of-them little to-me it-matters.

sentences.t:
Yo con los mios ferir [los] quiero delant,
vos con los vuestros firme mientre [a] la çaga tengades;
si cueta fuere, bien me podredes huviar.”

sentences.l:
I with the mine to-strike [them] want ahead,
you with the yours firmly [at] the rear may-you-hold;
if need there-shall-be, well me you-will-be-able to-help.”

sentences.t:
“Oy vos dix la missa de santa Trinidade.
Por esso salí de mi tierra e vin vos buscar[e],
por sabor que avía de algún moro matar[e];

sentences.l:
“Today to-you I-said the mass of holy Trinity.
For that I-went-out from my land and came you to-see[k],
for relish that I-had of some Moor to-kil[l];
